1
00:00:04,559 --> 00:00:09,478
As I was getting too big for Mr. Wopsle’s great-aunt’s room, my education under that

2
00:00:09,478 --> 00:00:15,199
preposterous female terminated. Not, however, until Biddy had imparted to me everything she

3
00:00:15,199 --> 00:00:21,799
knew, from the little catalogue of prices, to a comic song she had once bought for a half-penny.

4
00:00:21,799 --> 00:00:27,079
Although the only coherent part of the latter piece of literature were the opening lines,

5
00:00:27,079 --> 00:00:31,864
When I went to Lunnon town sirs, Too rul loo rul

6
00:00:31,864 --> 00:00:34,359
Too rul loo rul Wasn’t I done very brown sirs?

7
00:00:34,359 --> 00:00:37,158
Too rul loo rul Too rul loo rul

8
00:00:37,158 --> 00:00:43,158
—still, in my desire to be wiser, I got this composition by heart with the utmost gravity;

9
00:00:43,158 --> 00:00:47,879
nor do I recollect that I questioned its merit, except that I thought (as I still

10
00:00:47,879 --> 00:00:55,079
do) the amount of Too rul somewhat in excess of the poetry. In my hunger for information,

11
00:00:55,079 --> 00:01:00,439
I made proposals to Mr. Wopsle to bestow some intellectual crumbs upon me, with which he

12
00:01:00,439 --> 00:01:06,759
kindly complied. As it turned out, however, that he only wanted me for a dramatic lay-figure,

13
00:01:06,760 --> 00:01:11,120
to be contradicted and embraced and wept over and bullied and clutched and stabbed

14
00:01:11,120 --> 00:01:16,238
and knocked about in a variety of ways, I soon declined that course of instruction;

15
00:01:16,238 --> 00:01:21,318
though not until Mr. Wopsle in his poetic fury had severely mauled me.

16
00:01:21,318 --> 00:01:26,679
Whatever I acquired, I tried to impart to Joe. This statement sounds so well, that I cannot

17
00:01:26,680 --> 00:01:32,279
in my conscience let it pass unexplained. I wanted to make Joe less ignorant and common,

18
00:01:32,279 --> 00:01:38,359
that he might be worthier of my society and less open to Estella’s reproach.

19
00:01:38,359 --> 00:01:42,718
The old Battery out on the marshes was our place of study, and a broken slate

20
00:01:42,719 --> 00:01:47,760
and a short piece of slate-pencil were our educational implements: to which Joe always

21
00:01:47,760 --> 00:01:55,079
added a pipe of tobacco. I never knew Joe to remember anything from one Sunday to another,

22
00:01:55,079 --> 00:02:01,158
or to acquire, under my tuition, any piece of information whatever. Yet he would smoke

23
00:02:01,158 --> 00:02:06,079
his pipe at the Battery with a far more sagacious air than anywhere else,—even with

24
00:02:06,079 --> 00:02:13,840
a learned air,—as if he considered himself to be advancing immensely. Dear fellow, I hope he did.

25
00:02:13,840 --> 00:02:18,719
It was pleasant and quiet, out there with the sails on the river passing beyond the earthwork,

26
00:02:18,719 --> 00:02:23,959
and sometimes, when the tide was low, looking as if they belonged to sunken ships that were still

27
00:02:23,959 --> 00:02:29,398
sailing on at the bottom of the water. Whenever I watched the vessels standing

28
00:02:29,398 --> 00:02:35,199
out to sea with their white sails spread, I somehow thought of Miss Havisham and Estella;

29
00:02:35,199 --> 00:02:40,840
and whenever the light struck aslant, afar off, upon a cloud or sail or green hillside

30
00:02:40,840 --> 00:02:47,680
or water-line, it was just the same.—Miss Havisham and Estella and the strange house

31
00:02:47,680 --> 00:02:54,238
and the strange life appeared to have something to do with everything that was picturesque.

32
00:02:54,238 --> 00:03:01,519
One Sunday when Joe, greatly enjoying his pipe, had so plumed himself on being “most awful dull,”

33
00:03:01,520 --> 00:03:07,040
that I had given him up for the day, I lay on the earthwork for some time with my chin on my hand,

34
00:03:07,040 --> 00:03:13,680
descrying traces of Miss Havisham and Estella all over the prospect, in the sky and in the water,

35
00:03:13,680 --> 00:03:19,279
until at last I resolved to mention a thought concerning them that had been much in my head.

36
00:03:19,279 --> 00:03:24,079
“Joe,” said I; “don’t you think I ought to make Miss Havisham a visit?”

37
00:03:24,079 --> 00:03:29,920
“Well, Pip,” returned Joe, slowly considering. “What for?”

38
00:03:29,920 --> 00:03:34,199
“What for, Joe? What is any visit made for?”

39
00:03:34,199 --> 00:03:39,079
“There is some wisits p’r’aps,” said Joe, “as for ever remains open to the question, Pip. But in

40
00:03:39,079 --> 00:03:45,879
regard to wisiting Miss Havisham. She might think you wanted something,—expected something of her.”

41
00:03:45,879 --> 00:03:48,879
“Don’t you think I might say that I did not, Joe?”

42
00:03:48,879 --> 00:03:55,238
“You might, old chap,” said Joe. “And she might credit it. Similarly she mightn’t.”

43
00:03:55,238 --> 00:03:58,840
Joe felt, as I did, that he had made a point there,

44
00:03:58,840 --> 00:04:04,279
and he pulled hard at his pipe to keep himself from weakening it by repetition.

45
00:04:04,279 --> 00:04:08,638
“You see, Pip,” Joe pursued, as soon as he was past that danger,

46
00:04:08,639 --> 00:04:13,359
“Miss Havisham done the handsome thing by you. When Miss Havisham done the

47
00:04:13,359 --> 00:04:17,759
handsome thing by you, she called me back to say to me as that were all.”

48
00:04:17,760 --> 00:04:19,840
“Yes, Joe. I heard her.”

49
00:04:19,840 --> 00:04:22,959
“ALL,” Joe repeated, very emphatically.

50
00:04:22,959 --> 00:04:25,439
“Yes, Joe. I tell you, I heard her.”

51
00:04:25,439 --> 00:04:29,918
“Which I meantersay, Pip, it might be that her meaning were,—Make a end on

52
00:04:29,920 --> 00:04:36,600
it!—As you was!—Me to the North, and you to the South!—Keep in sunders!”

53
00:04:36,600 --> 00:04:39,799
I had thought of that too, and it was very far from comforting

54
00:04:39,799 --> 00:04:44,359
to me to find that he had thought of it; for it seemed to render it more probable.

55
00:04:44,359 --> 00:04:46,079
“But, Joe.”

56
00:04:46,079 --> 00:04:48,120
“Yes, old chap.”

57
00:04:48,120 --> 00:04:53,840
“Here am I, getting on in the first year of my time, and, since the day of my being bound,

58
00:04:53,840 --> 00:05:00,158
I have never thanked Miss Havisham, or asked after her, or shown that I remember her.”

59
00:05:00,158 --> 00:05:03,599
“That’s true, Pip; and unless you was to turn her out a set

60
00:05:03,600 --> 00:05:08,318
of shoes all four round,—and which I meantersay as even a set of shoes all

61
00:05:08,318 --> 00:05:14,359
four round might not be acceptable as a present, in a total wacancy of hoofs—”

62
00:05:14,359 --> 00:05:19,759
“I don’t mean that sort of remembrance, Joe; I don’t mean a present.”

63
00:05:19,760 --> 00:05:25,879
But Joe had got the idea of a present in his head and must harp upon it. “Or even,” said he,

64
00:05:25,879 --> 00:05:30,238
“if you was helped to knocking her up a new chain for the front door,—or say a gross or

65
00:05:30,238 --> 00:05:35,119
two of shark-headed screws for general use,—or some light fancy article, such

66
00:05:35,119 --> 00:05:40,920
as a toasting-fork when she took her muffins,—or a gridiron when she took a sprat or such like—”

67
00:05:40,920 --> 00:05:44,520
“I don’t mean any present at all, Joe,” I interposed.

68
00:05:44,520 --> 00:05:50,478
“Well,” said Joe, still harping on it as though I had particularly pressed it, “if I was yourself,

69
00:05:50,478 --> 00:05:55,799
Pip, I wouldn’t. No, I would not. For what’s a door-chain when she’s got one always up? And

70
00:05:55,799 --> 00:06:01,920
shark-headers is open to misrepresentations. And if it was a toasting-fork, you’d go into brass and

71
00:06:01,920 --> 00:06:07,478
do yourself no credit. And the oncommonest workman can’t show himself oncommon in a

72
00:06:07,478 --> 00:06:14,679
gridiron,—for a gridiron IS a gridiron,” said Joe, steadfastly impressing it upon me, as if

73
00:06:14,680 --> 00:06:19,959
he were endeavouring to rouse me from a fixed delusion, “and you may haim at what you like, but

74
00:06:19,959 --> 00:06:25,279
a gridiron it will come out, either by your leave or again your leave, and you can’t help yourself—”

75
00:06:25,279 --> 00:06:30,158
“My dear Joe,” I cried, in desperation, taking hold of his coat, “don’t go on

76
00:06:30,158 --> 00:06:35,119
in that way. I never thought of making Miss Havisham any present.”

77
00:06:35,119 --> 00:06:39,439
“No, Pip,” Joe assented, as if he had been contending for that, all along;

78
00:06:40,279 --> 00:06:44,439
“and what I say to you is, you are right, Pip.”

79
00:06:44,439 --> 00:06:50,119
“Yes, Joe; but what I wanted to say, was, that as we are rather slack just now, if you would

80
00:06:50,119 --> 00:06:58,238
give me a half-holiday to-morrow, I think I would go uptown and make a call on Miss Est—Havisham.”

81
00:06:58,238 --> 00:07:01,718
“Which her name,” said Joe, gravely, “ain’t Estavisham,

82
00:07:01,719 --> 00:07:04,959
Pip, unless she have been rechris’ened.”

83
00:07:04,959 --> 00:07:09,359
“I know, Joe, I know. It was a slip of mine. What do you think of it, Joe?”

84
00:07:10,840 --> 00:07:16,520
In brief, Joe thought that if I thought well of it, he thought well of it. But,

85
00:07:16,520 --> 00:07:20,760
he was particular in stipulating that if I were not received with cordiality,

86
00:07:20,760 --> 00:07:24,920
or if I were not encouraged to repeat my visit as a visit which had no ulterior

87
00:07:24,920 --> 00:07:30,079
object but was simply one of gratitude for a favour received, then this experimental

88
00:07:30,079 --> 00:07:36,398
trip should have no successor. By these conditions I promised to abide.

89
00:07:36,398 --> 00:07:41,199
Now, Joe kept a journeyman at weekly wages whose name was Orlick. He pretended

90
00:07:41,199 --> 00:07:46,558
that his Christian name was Dolge,—a clear Impossibility,—but he was a fellow of that

91
00:07:46,559 --> 00:07:51,959
obstinate disposition that I believe him to have been the prey of no delusion in this particular,

92
00:07:51,959 --> 00:07:58,119
but wilfully to have imposed that name upon the village as an affront to its understanding. He

93
00:07:58,119 --> 00:08:03,279
was a broadshouldered loose-limbed swarthy fellow of great strength, never in a hurry,

94
00:08:03,279 --> 00:08:09,519
and always slouching. He never even seemed to come to his work on purpose, but would slouch in as if

95
00:08:09,520 --> 00:08:16,000
by mere accident; and when he went to the Jolly Bargemen to eat his dinner, or went away at night,

96
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:22,119
he would slouch out, like Cain or the Wandering Jew, as if he had no idea where he was going and

97
00:08:22,119 --> 00:08:27,959
no intention of ever coming back. He lodged at a sluice-keeper’s out on the marshes,

98
00:08:27,959 --> 00:08:33,359
and on working-days would come slouching from his hermitage, with his hands in his pockets and his

99
00:08:33,359 --> 00:08:40,238
dinner loosely tied in a bundle round his neck and dangling on his back. On Sundays he mostly

100
00:08:40,238 --> 00:08:46,158
lay all day on the sluice-gates, or stood against ricks and barns. He always slouched,

101
00:08:46,158 --> 00:08:53,439
locomotively, with his eyes on the ground; and, when accosted or otherwise required to raise them,

102
00:08:53,439 --> 00:08:59,679
he looked up in a half-resentful, half-puzzled way, as though the only thought he ever had was,

103
00:08:59,680 --> 00:09:05,279
that it was rather an odd and injurious fact that he should never be thinking.

104
00:09:05,279 --> 00:09:10,759
This morose journeyman had no liking for me. When I was very small and timid,

105
00:09:10,760 --> 00:09:16,520
he gave me to understand that the Devil lived in a black corner of the forge, and that he knew the

106
00:09:16,520 --> 00:09:23,359
fiend very well: also that it was necessary to make up the fire, once in seven years,

107
00:09:23,359 --> 00:09:31,439
with a live boy, and that I might consider myself fuel. When I became Joe’s ’prentice, Orlick was

108
00:09:31,439 --> 00:09:38,838
perhaps confirmed in some suspicion that I should displace him; howbeit, he liked me still less.

109
00:09:38,840 --> 00:09:45,840
Not that he ever said anything, or did anything, openly importing hostility; I only noticed that he

110
00:09:45,840 --> 00:09:53,199
always beat his sparks in my direction, and that whenever I sang Old Clem, he came in out of time.

111
00:09:53,199 --> 00:10:00,000
Dolge Orlick was at work and present, next day, when I reminded Joe of my half-holiday.

112
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:04,959
He said nothing at the moment, for he and Joe had just got a piece of hot iron between them,

113
00:10:04,959 --> 00:10:09,158
and I was at the bellows; but by and by he said, leaning on his hammer,—

114
00:10:09,959 --> 00:10:15,238
“Now, master! Sure you’re not a-going to favour only one of us. If Young Pip has a

115
00:10:15,238 --> 00:10:21,119
half-holiday, do as much for Old Orlick.” I suppose he was about five-and-twenty,

116
00:10:21,119 --> 00:10:24,760
but he usually spoke of himself as an ancient person.

117
00:10:24,760 --> 00:10:29,040
“Why, what’ll you do with a half-holiday, if you get it?” said Joe.

118
00:10:29,040 --> 00:10:34,519
“What’ll I do with it! What’ll he do with it? I’ll do as much with it as him,” said Orlick.

119
00:10:34,520 --> 00:10:38,238
“As to Pip, he’s going up town,” said Joe.

120
00:10:38,238 --> 00:10:44,119
“Well then, as to Old Orlick, he’s a-going up town,” retorted that worthy. “Two can go up

121
00:10:44,119 --> 00:10:51,719
town. Tain’t only one wot can go up town. “Don’t lose your temper,” said Joe.

122
00:10:51,719 --> 00:10:56,359
“Shall if I like,” growled Orlick. “Some and their uptowning! Now,

123
00:10:56,359 --> 00:11:01,478
master! Come. No favouring in this shop. Be a man!”

124
00:11:01,478 --> 00:11:06,040
The master refusing to entertain the subject until the journeyman was in a better temper,

125
00:11:06,040 --> 00:11:10,879
Orlick plunged at the furnace, drew out a red-hot bar, made at me with it as if he were

126
00:11:10,879 --> 00:11:15,478
going to run it through my body, whisked it round my head, laid it on the anvil,

127
00:11:15,478 --> 00:11:21,519
hammered it out,—as if it were I, I thought, and the sparks were my spirting blood,—and finally

128
00:11:21,520 --> 00:11:27,799
said, when he had hammered himself hot and the iron cold, and he again leaned on his hammer,—

129
00:11:27,799 --> 00:11:29,600
“Now, master!”

130
00:11:29,600 --> 00:11:32,199
“Are you all right now?” demanded Joe.

131
00:11:32,199 --> 00:11:35,920
“Ah! I am all right,” said gruff Old Orlick.

132
00:11:35,920 --> 00:11:39,840
“Then, as in general you stick to your work as well as most men,” said Joe,

133
00:11:39,840 --> 00:11:42,719
“let it be a half-holiday for all.”

134
00:11:42,719 --> 00:11:47,199
My sister had been standing silent in the yard, within hearing,—she was a

135
00:11:47,199 --> 00:11:53,679
most unscrupulous spy and listener,—and she instantly looked in at one of the windows.

136
00:11:53,680 --> 00:12:00,719
“Like you, you fool!” said she to Joe, “giving holidays to great idle hulkers like that.

137
00:12:00,719 --> 00:12:07,559
You are a rich man, upon my life, to waste wages in that way. I wish I was his master!”

138
00:12:07,559 --> 00:12:13,158
“You’d be everybody’s master, if you durst,” retorted Orlick, with an ill-favoured grin.

139
00:12:13,158 --> 00:12:16,359
(“Let her alone,” said Joe.)

140
00:12:16,359 --> 00:12:22,398
“I’d be a match for all noodles and all rogues,” returned my sister, beginning to work herself into

141
00:12:22,398 --> 00:12:28,000
a mighty rage. “And I couldn’t be a match for the noodles, without being a match for your master,

142
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:33,600
who’s the dunder-headed king of the noodles. And I couldn’t be a match for the rogues, without being

143
00:12:33,600 --> 00:12:39,040
a match for you, who are the blackest-looking and the worst rogue between this and France. Now!”

144
00:12:40,279 --> 00:12:43,238
“You’re a foul shrew, Mother Gargery,” growled the

145
00:12:43,238 --> 00:12:47,959
journeyman. “If that makes a judge of rogues, you ought to be a good’un.”

146
00:12:47,959 --> 00:12:50,639
(“Let her alone, will you?” said Joe.)

147
00:12:50,639 --> 00:12:56,279
“What did you say?” cried my sister, beginning to scream. “What did you say? What did that

148
00:12:56,279 --> 00:13:03,519
fellow Orlick say to me, Pip? What did he call me, with my husband standing by? Oh! oh! oh!”

149
00:13:03,520 --> 00:13:08,478
Each of these exclamations was a shriek; and I must remark of my sister, what is equally

150
00:13:08,478 --> 00:13:14,040
true of all the violent women I have ever seen, that passion was no excuse for her,

151
00:13:14,040 --> 00:13:19,238
because it is undeniable that instead of lapsing into passion, she consciously and deliberately

152
00:13:19,238 --> 00:13:26,478
took extraordinary pains to force herself into it, and became blindly furious by regular stages;

153
00:13:26,478 --> 00:13:33,078
“what was the name he gave me before the base man who swore to defend me? Oh! Hold me! Oh!”

154
00:13:33,079 --> 00:13:39,279
“Ah-h-h!” growled the journeyman, between his teeth, “I’d hold you, if you was my wife.

155
00:13:39,279 --> 00:13:42,718
I’d hold you under the pump, and choke it out of you.”

156
00:13:42,719 --> 00:13:46,279
(“I tell you, let her alone,” said Joe.)

157
00:13:46,279 --> 00:13:51,359
“Oh! To hear him!” cried my sister, with a clap of her hands and a scream together,—which

158
00:13:51,359 --> 00:13:57,199
was her next stage. “To hear the names he’s giving me! That Orlick! In my own house! Me,

159
00:13:57,199 --> 00:14:03,638
a married woman! With my husband standing by! Oh! Oh!” Here my sister, after a fit of clappings and

160
00:14:03,639 --> 00:14:08,600
screamings, beat her hands upon her bosom and upon her knees, and threw her cap off,

161
00:14:08,600 --> 00:14:15,318
and pulled her hair down,—which were the last stages on her road to frenzy. Being by this time

162
00:14:15,318 --> 00:14:22,439
a perfect Fury and a complete success, she made a dash at the door which I had fortunately locked.

163
00:14:22,439 --> 00:14:27,918
What could the wretched Joe do now, after his disregarded parenthetical interruptions,

164
00:14:27,920 --> 00:14:33,719
but stand up to his journeyman, and ask him what he meant by interfering betwixt himself and Mrs.

165
00:14:33,719 --> 00:14:40,959
Joe; and further whether he was man enough to come on? Old Orlick felt that the situation

166
00:14:40,959 --> 00:14:47,040
admitted of nothing less than coming on, and was on his defence straightway; so, without so much

167
00:14:47,040 --> 00:14:54,478
as pulling off their singed and burnt aprons, they went at one another, like two giants. But,

168
00:14:54,478 --> 00:15:02,238
if any man in that neighbourhood could stand uplong against Joe, I never saw the man. Orlick,

169
00:15:02,238 --> 00:15:06,760
as if he had been of no more account than the pale young gentleman, was very soon among the

170
00:15:06,760 --> 00:15:13,439
coal-dust, and in no hurry to come out of it. Then Joe unlocked the door and picked up my sister,

171
00:15:13,439 --> 00:15:18,558
who had dropped insensible at the window (but who had seen the fight first, I think), and who

172
00:15:18,559 --> 00:15:23,799
was carried into the house and laid down, and who was recommended to revive, and would do nothing

173
00:15:23,799 --> 00:15:30,398
but struggle and clench her hands in Joe’s hair. Then came that singular calm and silence which

174
00:15:30,398 --> 00:15:37,439
succeed all uproars; and then, with the vague sensation which I have always connected with such

175
00:15:37,439 --> 00:15:46,238
a lull,—namely, that it was Sunday, and somebody was dead,—I went upstairs to dress myself.

176
00:15:46,238 --> 00:15:49,840
When I came down again, I found Joe and Orlick sweeping up,

177
00:15:49,840 --> 00:15:54,439
without any other traces of discomposure than a slit in one of Orlick’s nostrils,

178
00:15:54,439 --> 00:16:00,478
which was neither expressive nor ornamental. A pot of beer had appeared from the Jolly Bargemen,

179
00:16:00,478 --> 00:16:05,959
and they were sharing it by turns in a peaceable manner. The lull had a sedative and philosophical

180
00:16:05,959 --> 00:16:11,079
influence on Joe, who followed me out into the road to say, as a parting observation

181
00:16:11,079 --> 00:16:18,398
that might do me good, “On the Rampage, Pip, and off the Rampage, Pip:—such is Life!”

182
00:16:18,398 --> 00:16:22,799
With what absurd emotions (for we think the feelings that are very serious in a man quite

183
00:16:22,799 --> 00:16:29,520
comical in a boy) I found myself again going to Miss Havisham’s, matters little here. Nor,

184
00:16:29,520 --> 00:16:36,040
how I passed and repassed the gate many times before I could make up my mind to ring. Nor,

185
00:16:36,040 --> 00:16:41,959
how I debated whether I should go away without ringing; nor, how I should undoubtedly have gone,

186
00:16:41,959 --> 00:16:50,600
if my time had been my own, to come back. Miss Sarah Pocket came to the gate. No Estella.

187
00:16:50,600 --> 00:16:55,478
“How, then? You here again?” said Miss Pocket. “What do you want?”

188
00:16:55,478 --> 00:17:01,079
When I said that I only came to see how Miss Havisham was, Sarah evidently deliberated whether

189
00:17:01,079 --> 00:17:07,318
or no she should send me about my business. But unwilling to hazard the responsibility,

190
00:17:07,318 --> 00:17:12,638
she let me in, and presently brought the sharp message that I was to “come up.”

191
00:17:12,639 --> 00:17:17,398
Everything was unchanged, and Miss Havisham was alone.

192
00:17:17,398 --> 00:17:25,079
“Well?” said she, fixing her eyes upon me. “I hope you want nothing? You’ll get nothing.”

193
00:17:25,079 --> 00:17:29,478
“No indeed, Miss Havisham. I only wanted you to know that I am doing

194
00:17:29,478 --> 00:17:33,799
very well in my apprenticeship, and am always much obliged to you.”

195
00:17:33,799 --> 00:17:38,760
“There, there!” with the old restless fingers. “Come now and then;

196
00:17:38,760 --> 00:17:43,000
come on your birthday.—Ay!” she cried suddenly,

197
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:49,439
turning herself and her chair towards me, “You are looking round for Estella? Hey?”

198
00:17:49,439 --> 00:17:51,359
I had been looking round,—in fact,

199
00:17:51,359 --> 00:17:55,318
for Estella,—and I stammered that I hoped she was well.

200
00:17:55,318 --> 00:18:01,638
“Abroad,” said Miss Havisham; “educating for a lady; far out of reach; prettier than ever;

201
00:18:01,639 --> 00:18:06,799
admired by all who see her. Do you feel that you have lost her?”

202
00:18:06,799 --> 00:18:11,359
There was such a malignant enjoyment in her utterance of the last words,

203
00:18:11,359 --> 00:18:16,359
and she broke into such a disagreeable laugh, that I was at a loss what to say.

204
00:18:16,359 --> 00:18:21,679
She spared me the trouble of considering, by dismissing me. When the gate was closed upon

205
00:18:21,680 --> 00:18:28,158
me by Sarah of the walnut-shell countenance, I felt more than ever dissatisfied with my home

206
00:18:28,158 --> 00:18:34,519
and with my trade and with everything; and that was all I took by that motion.

207
00:18:34,520 --> 00:18:40,238
As I was loitering along the High Street, looking in disconsolately at the shop windows,

208
00:18:40,238 --> 00:18:43,599
and thinking what I would buy if I were a gentleman, who should come out of the

209
00:18:43,600 --> 00:18:51,600
bookshop but Mr. Wopsle. Mr. Wopsle had in his hand the affecting tragedy of George Barnwell,

210
00:18:51,600 --> 00:18:56,680
in which he had that moment invested sixpence, with the view of heaping every word of it on

211
00:18:56,680 --> 00:19:03,439
the head of Pumblechook, with whom he was going to drink tea. No sooner did he see me, than he

212
00:19:03,439 --> 00:19:08,238
appeared to consider that a special Providence had put a ’prentice in his way to be read at;

213
00:19:08,238 --> 00:19:13,879
and he laid hold of me, and insisted on my accompanying him to the Pumblechookian parlour.

214
00:19:13,879 --> 00:19:18,840
As I knew it would be miserable at home, and as the nights were dark and the way was dreary,

215
00:19:18,840 --> 00:19:24,680
and almost any companionship on the road was better than none, I made no great resistance;

216
00:19:24,680 --> 00:19:30,920
consequently, we turned into Pumblechook’s just as the street and the shops were lighting up.

217
00:19:30,920 --> 00:19:36,040
As I never assisted at any other representation of George Barnwell, I don’t know how long it

218
00:19:36,040 --> 00:19:41,799
may usually take; but I know very well that it took until half-past nine o’ clock that night,

219
00:19:41,799 --> 00:19:47,119
and that when Mr. Wopsle got into Newgate, I thought he never would go to the scaffold,

220
00:19:47,119 --> 00:19:51,920
he became so much slower than at any former period of his disgraceful career. I thought

221
00:19:51,920 --> 00:19:56,719
it a little too much that he should complain of being cut short in his flower after all,

222
00:19:56,719 --> 00:20:03,359
as if he had not been running to seed, leaf after leaf, ever since his course began. This, however,

223
00:20:03,359 --> 00:20:09,799
was a mere question of length and wearisomeness. What stung me, was the identification of the whole

224
00:20:09,799 --> 00:20:14,719
affair with my unoffending self. When Barnwell began to go wrong, I declare

225
00:20:14,719 --> 00:20:21,359
that I felt positively apologetic, Pumblechook’s indignant stare so taxed me with it. Wopsle, too,

226
00:20:21,359 --> 00:20:27,718
took pains to present me in the worst light. At once ferocious and maudlin, I was made to murder

227
00:20:27,719 --> 00:20:33,040
my uncle with no extenuating circumstances whatever; Millwood put me down in argument,

228
00:20:33,040 --> 00:20:38,719
on every occasion; it became sheer monomania in my master’s daughter to care a button for me;

229
00:20:38,719 --> 00:20:43,359
and all I can say for my gasping and procrastinating conduct on the fatal morning,

230
00:20:43,359 --> 00:20:50,000
is, that it was worthy of the general feebleness of my character. Even after I was happily hanged

231
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:55,398
and Wopsle had closed the book, Pumblechook sat staring at me, and shaking his head, and saying,

232
00:20:55,398 --> 00:21:01,599
“Take warning, boy, take warning!” as if it were a well-known fact that I contemplated murdering a

233
00:21:01,600 --> 00:21:07,840
near relation, provided I could only induce one to have the weakness to become my benefactor.

234
00:21:07,840 --> 00:21:14,398
It was a very dark night when it was all over, and when I set out with Mr. Wopsle on the walk home.

235
00:21:14,398 --> 00:21:21,759
Beyond town, we found a heavy mist out, and it fell wet and thick. The turnpike lamp was a blur,

236
00:21:21,760 --> 00:21:27,879
quite out of the lamp’s usual place apparently, and its rays looked solid substance on the fog.

237
00:21:27,879 --> 00:21:32,680
We were noticing this, and saying how that the mist rose with a change of wind from a certain

238
00:21:32,680 --> 00:21:38,440
quarter of our marshes, when we came upon a man, slouching under the lee of the turnpike house.

239
00:21:39,119 --> 00:21:47,680
“Halloa!” we said, stopping. “Orlick there?” “Ah!” he answered, slouching out. “I was standing

240
00:21:47,680 --> 00:21:53,279
by a minute, on the chance of company.” “You are late,” I remarked.

241
00:21:53,279 --> 00:21:58,040
Orlick not unnaturally answered, “Well? And you’re late.”

242
00:21:58,040 --> 00:22:03,799
“We have been,” said Mr. Wopsle, exalted with his late performance,—“we have been indulging,

243
00:22:03,799 --> 00:22:06,840
Mr. Orlick, in an intellectual evening.”

244
00:22:06,840 --> 00:22:12,559
Old Orlick growled, as if he had nothing to say about that, and we all went on together.

245
00:22:12,559 --> 00:22:17,600
I asked him presently whether he had been spending his half-holiday up and down town?

246
00:22:17,600 --> 00:22:23,119
“Yes,” said he, “all of it. I come in behind yourself. I didn’t see you,

247
00:22:23,119 --> 00:22:28,959
but I must have been pretty close behind you. By the by, the guns is going again.”

248
00:22:28,959 --> 00:22:31,279
“At the Hulks?” said I.

249
00:22:31,279 --> 00:22:36,119
“Ay! There’s some of the birds flown from the cages. The guns have been going since dark,

250
00:22:36,119 --> 00:22:38,318
about. You’ll hear one presently.”

251
00:22:39,119 --> 00:22:44,719
In effect, we had not walked many yards further, when the well-remembered boom came towards us,

252
00:22:44,719 --> 00:22:49,959
deadened by the mist, and heavily rolled away along the low grounds by the river,

253
00:22:49,959 --> 00:22:54,359
as if it were pursuing and threatening the fugitives.

254
00:22:54,359 --> 00:22:58,439
“A good night for cutting off in,” said Orlick. “We’d be puzzled how

255
00:22:58,439 --> 00:23:01,599
to bring down a jail-bird on the wing, to-night.”

256
00:23:01,600 --> 00:23:07,680
The subject was a suggestive one to me, and I thought about it in silence. Mr. Wopsle, as the

257
00:23:07,680 --> 00:23:14,559
ill-requited uncle of the evening’s tragedy, fell to meditating aloud in his garden at Camberwell.

258
00:23:14,559 --> 00:23:21,559
Orlick, with his hands in his pockets, slouched heavily at my side. It was very dark, very wet,

259
00:23:21,559 --> 00:23:28,680
very muddy, and so we splashed along. Now and then, the sound of the signal cannon broke upon us

260
00:23:28,680 --> 00:23:35,238
again, and again rolled sulkily along the course of the river. I kept myself to myself and my

261
00:23:35,238 --> 00:23:42,519
thoughts. Mr. Wopsle died amiably at Camberwell, and exceedingly game on Bosworth Field, and in the

262
00:23:42,520 --> 00:23:48,318
greatest agonies at Glastonbury. Orlick sometimes growled, “Beat it out, beat it out,—Old Clem! With

263
00:23:48,318 --> 00:23:54,079
a clink for the stout,—Old Clem!” I thought he had been drinking, but he was not drunk.

264
00:23:54,079 --> 00:23:59,558
Thus, we came to the village. The way by which we approached it took us past the Three Jolly

265
00:23:59,559 --> 00:24:05,959
Bargemen, which we were surprised to find—it being eleven o’clock—in a state of commotion,

266
00:24:05,959 --> 00:24:11,520
with the door wide open, and unwonted lights that had been hastily caught up and put down

267
00:24:11,520 --> 00:24:16,478
scattered about. Mr. Wopsle dropped in to ask what was the matter (surmising

268
00:24:16,478 --> 00:24:21,119
that a convict had been taken), but came running out in a great hurry.

269
00:24:21,119 --> 00:24:26,799
“There’s something wrong,” said he, without stopping, “up at your place, Pip. Run all!”

270
00:24:26,799 --> 00:24:32,079
“What is it?” I asked, keeping up with him. So did Orlick, at my side.

271
00:24:32,079 --> 00:24:36,600
“I can’t quite understand. The house seems to have been violently entered when Joe

272
00:24:36,600 --> 00:24:42,359
Gargery was out. Supposed by convicts. Somebody has been attacked and hurt.”

273
00:24:42,359 --> 00:24:46,759
We were running too fast to admit of more being said, and we made no stop until we

274
00:24:46,760 --> 00:24:53,760
got into our kitchen. It was full of people; the whole village was there, or in the yard;

275
00:24:53,760 --> 00:24:58,318
and there was a surgeon, and there was Joe, and there were a group of women,

276
00:24:58,318 --> 00:25:04,879
all on the floor in the midst of the kitchen. The unemployed bystanders drew back when they saw me,

277
00:25:04,879 --> 00:25:10,439
and so I became aware of my sister,—lying without sense or movement on the bare boards

278
00:25:10,439 --> 00:25:14,959
where she had been knocked down by a tremendous blow on the back of the head, dealt by some

279
00:25:14,959 --> 00:25:21,799
unknown hand when her face was turned towards the fire,—destined never to be on the Rampage again,

280
00:25:21,799 --> 00:25:25,000
while she was the wife of Joe. Chapter 16.
